From: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mt7993: fire PATCH_FINISH_REQ without waiting for a reply

The 20 s wait on 0x07 is the whole failure.  Reading the endpoint's config
space live, not through the cached resource file, shows both functions coming
out of PATCH_FINISH_REQ with command=0x0000 and BAR0=0x00000004: the chip
cleared its own PCIe configuration, so every MMIO read after that walks off a
mapping nobody decodes any more and returns noise.  That is why diag[post-07]
prints 0xcd0c1375 instead of a register, why the semaphore release times out
again, and why a second bring-up cannot get driver own.

The chip is not dead.  Writing BAR0 back from the shell and setting
PCI_COMMAND to memory plus bus master made it answer again: the next ifup
immediately picked up a reply that had been sitting in the RX ring the whole
time, read sem=2, pushed 0x05, took all four scatter descriptors and reached
0x07 again.  So the download is intact and the endpoint reset is not a jump
into a faulting image - it is what the ROM does when the host stops talking.

Nothing else in the flow waits like this.  The scatter is fire and forget,
mt_wifi sends PATCH_FINISH_REQ with RSP_WAIT_MS_TIME(0) and lets
CmdPatchFinishRsp run whenever the answer turns up, and upstream mt76 follows
start_patch straight away with the semaphore release.  Do the same: send 0x07,
do not wait, and let the semaphore release be the first thing that has to be
read back.  diag_jump_trace keeps the 400 ms after the send readable, sampling
PCI_COMMAND and the two ROM state registers every 10 ms and stopping at the
first sample where the endpoint dropped, so the next boot either shows the
flow running past 0x07 or names the millisecond the chip went away.

Signed-off-by: chasey-dev <ellenyoung0912@gmail.com>
---
--- a/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu_wm.c
+++ b/wlan_hwifi/mcu/mcu_wm.c
@@ -240,6 +240,86 @@
 module_param(diag_crc_check, bool, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(diag_crc_check, "let the ROM verify the patch CRC");
 
+/*
+ * 048: PATCH_FINISH_REQ is fire and forget.
+ *
+ * Waiting for a reply to 0x07 is what kills the bring-up.  On the vendor board
+ * the answer comes back immediately; here the poll sits for the full 20 s, and
+ * by the time it gives up both PCI functions have command=0x0000 and BAR0=0,
+ * so every read afterwards walks off the mapping and returns noise.  The chip
+ * is not dead at that point: restoring BAR0 and PCI_COMMAND from the shell was
+ * enough to make it hand over a reply that had been queued all along, and a
+ * second ifup then read sem=2, pushed 0x05, took four scatter descriptors and
+ * reached 0x07 again - so the download itself is intact and only the wait is
+ * wrong.
+ *
+ * mt_wifi does not wait either: MtCmdPatchFinishReq sets RSP_WAIT_MS_TIME(0)
+ * and lets CmdPatchFinishRsp run whenever the answer shows up.  That is the
+ * model used here.  The scatter is fire and forget, 0x07 is fire and forget,
+ * and the ROM's own semaphore reply after the release is the first thing that
+ * has to be read back, exactly like upstream mt76 where start_patch is
+ * followed straight away by the semaphore release.
+ *
+ * diag_jump_trace keeps the 400 ms window readable: it samples PCI_COMMAND
+ * and the two ROM state registers every 10 ms and stops at the first sample
+ * where the endpoint dropped, which is the difference between "the patch runs
+ * and the host is simply not listening" and "the image faults on entry".
+ */
+static bool diag_jump_trace;
+module_param(diag_jump_trace, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(diag_jump_trace,
+		 "trace the endpoint for 400 ms after PATCH_FINISH_REQ");
+
+#define DIAG48_LPCTL_ADDR	0x000E0010
+#define DIAG48_ON_MISC_ADDR	0x000E00F0
+
+static bool
+mcu_diag_jump_trace(struct mtk_hw_dev *dev)
+{
+	struct mtk_bus_trans *trans = dev->bus_trans;
+	struct pci_dev *pdev = to_pci_dev(dev->dev);
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < 40; i++) {
+		u16 cmd = 0xffff;
+
+		pci_read_config_word(pdev, PCI_COMMAND, &cmd);
+		dev_err(dev->dev,
+			"diag trace[%02d]: cmd=0x%04x lpctl=0x%08x on_misc=0x%08x\n",
+			i, cmd, bus_read(trans, DIAG48_LPCTL_ADDR),
+			bus_read(trans, DIAG48_ON_MISC_ADDR));
+
+		if (cmd != (PCI_COMMAND_MEMORY | PCI_COMMAND_MASTER)) {
+			dev_err(dev->dev,
+				"diag trace: endpoint dropped after %d ms\n",
+				i * 10);
+			return true;
+		}
+
+		msleep(10);
+	}
+
+	return false;
+}
+
+static void
+mcu_diag_restore_pci(struct mtk_hw_dev *dev)
+{
+	struct pci_dev *pdev = to_pci_dev(dev->dev);
+	resource_size_t bar0 = pci_resource_start(pdev, 0);
+
+	pci_write_config_dword(pdev, PCI_BASE_ADDRESS_0,
+			       lower_32_bits(bar0));
+	pci_write_config_word(pdev, PCI_COMMAND,
+			      PCI_COMMAND_MEMORY | PCI_COMMAND_MASTER);
+
+	dev_err(dev->dev,
+		"diag: BAR0 back to 0x%llx, lpctl=0x%08x on_misc=0x%08x\n",
+		(unsigned long long)bar0,
+		bus_read(dev->bus_trans, DIAG48_LPCTL_ADDR),
+		bus_read(dev->bus_trans, DIAG48_ON_MISC_ADDR));
+}
+
 static int
 mcu_wm_start_patch(struct mtk_hw_dev *dev)
 {
@@ -255,14 +335,22 @@
 		.cmd = MCU_CMD_PATCH_FINISH_REQ,
 		.data = &req,
 		.len = sizeof(req),
-		.wait_resp = true,
+		.wait_resp = false,
 		.dest = MCU_DEST_WM,
 		.resp = NULL,
 	};
 
 	req.check_crc = diag_crc_check ? 1 : 0;
 
-	return mtk_mcu_tx_nocheck(dev, &mcu_txblk);
+	if (mtk_mcu_tx_nocheck(dev, &mcu_txblk)) {
+		dev_err(dev->dev, "Failed to send PATCH_FINISH_REQ\n");
+		return -EIO;
+	}
+
+	if (diag_jump_trace && mcu_diag_jump_trace(dev))
+		mcu_diag_restore_pci(dev);
+
+	return 0;
 }
 
 static u32 mcu_wm_gen_patch_dl_mode(u32 sec_info)
